  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nA fairly typical and benign summer-time setup can be expected the \nfirst full week of August as South Texas lies on the eastern flank \nof mid-lvl ridging over the desert SW. At the lower-lvls S-SE flow \nwill gradually increase towards the mid-week period as the \nsubtropical highs builds westward into the Gulf. Outside of \nlocalized moisture pooling along the sea-breeze, ambient PWATs will \ngenerally run 1.4 to 1.8 inches through the period (generally 25th \nto 50th percentile for climo). This setup will maintain largely dry \nconditions with any storm chances confined to the sea breeze. Rain \nchances will generally be 10 percent or less (but not entirely zero) \nthrough Wednesday, before potentially creeping up slightly (mainly \nover the Victoria Crossroads) late week into the weekend as low-lvl \nsoutherly flow increases. \n\nAs mentioned in previous discussions we will remain seasonably warm \nthrough the forecast period with high temperatures largely in the \ntriple digits west of US-281, and 90s elsewhere.\nThese temperatures combined with lows in the upper 70s to lower 80s \nwill maintain a moderate to major heat risk each day. Max heat \nindices will also flirt with heat advisory criteria each day in the \nmid-week period and short-fused advisories will need to be \nconsidered. Finally, for anyone doing strenuous activities in direct \nsunlight, WBGT values will peak in the high to extreme range, \noccasionally even exceeding 90 degrees. To sum it all up, make sure \nto stay hydrated, take frequent cooling breaks, and seek shade when \npossible as we are entering the hottest time of the year \nclimatologically in South Texas.",
